# LAN entry point for the dashboard — HTTPS only. Caddy owns host port 8787
# on all interfaces, terminates TLS with a certificate from its own internal
# CA, and demands basic auth before proxying to the dashboard on the internal
# compose network (started by the `lan` profile — see docker-compose.yml and
# deploy/DEPLOY.md).
#
# The certificate covers both names below: `utility` for plain LAN clients
# (add a hosts entry if your router does not resolve it) and the Tailscale
# MagicDNS name for tailnet devices. `tls internal` issues it locally, so the
# names do not need to resolve anywhere for the cert to exist — but the name
# you type in the browser must match one of these SANs.
#
# Trust the CA on each client so browsers stop warning:
#   1. Get the CA root:
#        docker exec <project>-caddy-1 cat /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt
#      (<project> is your folder name with the ectowatch service appended —
#      run `docker compose ps` if you're not sure of the exact container name.)
#   2. Install it in the device's trust store (see deploy/DEPLOY.md).
#
# Change the password:
#   docker run --rm caddy:2-alpine caddy hash-password --plaintext 'newpass'
# then replace the hash below and `docker compose up -d --build` (--lan).
https://utility:8787, https://utility.example.ts.net:8787 {
	tls internal
	basic_auth {
		alan <bcrypt-hash-generate-your-own>
	}
	reverse_proxy ectowatch:8787
}
